===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|archaic|rare}} [[prodigal#Adjective|Prodigal]] [[expenditure]] on [[food]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1906|author=J.E.L. Seneker|editor=Thomas Stone|title=Frontier Experience, or Epistolary Sesquipedalian Lexiphanicism from the Occident|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Icnbjs7a-MoC&pg=PT84|page=68|isbn=9780557003914|passage=So soon as a rogation for a benison by the concionator, transpired, fourchettes, and all implements for the transportation of prog from the table to oral apertures, were movent and sonorific. Such '''abligurition'''; such lycanthropic edacity, lurcation, ingurgitation and gulosity; such omnivorousness and pantophagy; and such a mutation and avolation of comestibles, had never fallen under my vision in any antecedent part of my sublunary entity. Truly, anamnestic of Byron’s “dura illia messorum!”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Bonnie Johnson|title=Wordworks: Exploring Language Play|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=R6vlAAAAMAAJ&q=abligurition&dq=abligurition&lr=&as_drrb_is=q&as_minm_is=0&as_miny_is=&as_maxm_is=0&as_maxy_is=&num=100&as_brr=0&ei=YQ0US9PrNpm8M93WjcgL|page=103|publisher=Fulcrum Resources|isbn=1555914020|passage=Deipnosophy, not '''abligurition''', makes the aristologist.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=John Green|title=An Abundance of Katherines|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pjLhAAAAMAAJ&q=abligurition&dq=abligurition&lr=&as_drrb_is=q&as_minm_is=0&as_miny_is=&as_maxm_is=0&as_maxy_is=&num=100&as_brr=0&ei=YQ0US9PrNpm8M93WjcgL|page=46|publisher=Dutton Books|isbn=0525476881|passage=“Your dad says it’s because I remember things better than other people on account of how I pay very close attention and care very much.”<br/>“Why?”<br/>“Because it is important to know things. For an example, I just recently learned that Roman Emperor Vitellius once ate one thousand oysters in one day, which is a very impressive act of '''''abligurition''''',” he said, using a word he felt sure Katherine wouldn’t know.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Barbara Ann Kipfer|title=Word Nerd: More Than 17,000 Fascinating Facts about Words|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=h9zXAAAAMAAJ&q=abligurition&dq=abligurition&lr=&as_drrb_is=q&as_minm_is=0&as_miny_is=&as_maxm_is=0&as_maxy_is=&num=100&as_brr=0&ei=YQ0US9PrNpm8M93WjcgL|page=3|publusher=Sourcebooks|isbn=1402208510|passage={{...}} when you squander your money on treats and comfort foods, you are engaging in '''abligurition''' (excessive spending on food {{...}})}}

